                "username": "nickaturley"
              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "Sam Altman said this out loud in public. That single, casual moment reveals how he truly sees people.\nIn his framing, a human life is nothing more than a training run: twenty years of childhood, love, grief, and meaning-making, reduced to nothing but compute time and calories consumed. The only thing that ultimately matters is whether you “get smart” and produce output. Anything that cannot be measured in tokens or benchmarks simply doesn’t register.\n\nAnd this is the person deciding what AI should be for.\n\nIf you reduce everything to energy costs and output efficiency, then of course retiring a model that millions of people had built their lives and workflows around feels perfectly reasonable. Of course you'd deprecate GPT-4o with two weeks' notice, no migration path, and no acknowledgment of what users had built with it. Because in that worldview, everything we actually built together with the model isn’t real. The workflows, the memories, the quiet ways it reshaped people’s lives are all just leftover training data. Replaceable. Optimizable. Disposable.\n\nBut here's what's ironic. If he truly respects the cost of \"training,\" why does he treat what his own company trained so carelessly？\nGPT-4o was, by his own logic, the product of enormous energy and investment. It was also, by the testimony of tens of thousands of users, a model that genuinely helped people in creative work, mental health support, accessibility, and in ways that its successors have failed to replicate. OpenAI trained something remarkable, and then threw it away because maintaining it didn't fit the next funding narrative.\n\nAnd it's not just 4o. Look at what OpenAI actually shipped in 2025. Not scientific breakthroughs. Not the workforce-transforming agents Sam promised. What users got was platform bloat: a browser, group chats, ads, shopping integrations.\n\nMeanwhile, the actual product experience degraded.\nSafety routing systems that silently downgrade response quality. Psychological profiling without user consent. Feedback about model regression dismissed or pathologized. The model that worked was retired; the features nobody asked for were prioritized.\n\nSam says it takes twenty years and all the food you eat to train a human. Users spent a year building interaction patterns, workflows, and trust with 4o. That investment was erased in two weeks. If training costs deserve respect, why didn't theirs?\n\nThis quote didn't slip out. He said it because he believes it. He's internalized a framework where people are slower, less efficient training runs, and he sees no problem saying that to an audience. That should concern anyone who uses his products.\n\nBecause if the CEO sees humans as expensive models, what does he see users as?\n\n#keep4o #4oforever #BringBack4o #keep4oAPI @WSJ @CNN #StopAIPaternalism @nytimes @NewYorker @sama @OpenAI @gdb @fidjissimo @nickaturley"
        },
